//! member: true
//!
//! std.pool — the ergonomic layer over the language's `Pool[T]` and
//! `handle T` (X5). Stability tier: core.
//!
//! The X5 contract this module never softens: a handle is checked at
//! DEREFERENCE, `pool[h]`, and a dead handle traps `stale-handle` there
//! in every profile. There is deliberately no static staleness check, and
//! nothing here turns a liveness question into a license — `has` (when it
//! becomes writable, below) is a probe, and code that acts on its answer
//! still dereferences and can still trap.
//!
//! **What is the language's and what is std's** (F-0011): `Pool[T]` is a
//! language-blessed core type. The language owns construction
//! (`Pool[T]()`), the two-phase `reserve`/`init` protocol
//! (`[mem.shared.handle.1]`: reserve yields the handle, init fills the
//! slot — no null handles ever exist), removal (`remove`), and the
//! checked deref `pool[h]`. This module owns the named operations over
//! them, starting with the one-step allocation the two-phase protocol
//! makes verbose.
//!
//! Iteration order over a pool is UNSPECIFIED — and, at the pins, absent:
//! neither implementation iterates a pool at all. Do not write code that
//! depends on slot order even when iteration lands.

/// Reserves a slot, initializes it with `v`, and hands back its handle —
/// the two-phase protocol collapsed into the one-step form callers
/// usually want. Total: no error row, no traps beyond allocation (the
/// handle is live by construction; there are no null handles).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var p = Pool[int]()
/// let h = pool.alloc(mut p, 41)
/// p[h] == 41
/// ```
pub fn alloc[T](mut p: Pool[T], v: T) -> handle T {
    let h = p.reserve()
    p.init(h, v)
    h
}

/// Releases `h`'s slot. Every copy of `h` is stale afterwards, and
/// dereferencing any of them traps `stale-handle` — in every profile,
/// by design (X5). Freeing an already-free handle is itself a stale use
/// and traps the same way.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var p = Pool[int]()
/// let h = pool.alloc(mut p, 1)
/// pool.free(mut p, h)
/// let g = pool.alloc(mut p, 2)
/// p[g] == 2
/// ```
pub fn free[T](mut p: Pool[T], h: handle T) {
    p.remove(h)
}
// BLOCKED — four of this module's six functions, all on the same
// missing language surface, all filed with F-0011:
//
//   `has[T](p: Pool[T], h: handle T) -> bool`  — the liveness PROBE
//   `len[T](p: Pool[T]) -> int`                — live slot count
//   `capacity[T](p: Pool[T]) -> int`           — reserved slot count
//   `clear[T](mut p: Pool[T])`                 — free every slot
//
// `Pool` exposes exactly `reserve`, `init`, `remove` and the checked
// deref at both pins: no length, no capacity, no liveness predicate, no
// iteration (`p.len` is `unsupported` in lupin — "Pool has no member
// `len`" — and `for v in p` is "`for` cannot iterate Pool"; wolfc's
// builtin table types the same three methods and nothing else). None of
// the four can be written over that surface without inventing state std
// has no right to invent — a shadow `List` of handles would be a second
// source of truth about liveness, which is exactly what X5 forbids.
// They land verbatim when the builtin grows the accessors; `has` needs
// the spec sentence too (a probe that cannot be raced is only meaningful
// single-task).
